import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,a5 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CsGesDJn.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Bounded-memory Facebook crawler","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"feature/crawler-bounded-memory/operations.md","filePath":"feature/crawler-bounded-memory/operations.md"}'),n={name:"feature/crawler-bounded-memory/operations.md"};function s(i,e,c,d,l,p){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="bounded-memory-facebook-crawler" tabindex="-1">Bounded-memory Facebook crawler <a class="header-anchor" href="#bounded-memory-facebook-crawler" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bounded-memory Facebook crawler&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Facebook runs use a supervisor and one disposable browser worker at a time. Feed and results are checkpointed in <code>&lt;output&gt;.crawl.sqlite3</code>; comments are spooled to temporary SQLite storage. Comment workers process at most 25 posts before exiting, releasing Python and Chromium memory. Feed collection is a separate phase and is not limited to 25 posts. The API and scheduler remain running.</p><p>Defaults (see AI-service <code>.env.example</code>):</p><ul><li><code>CRAWLER_ISOLATED_BATCHES=true</code></li><li><code>CRAWLER_BATCH_SIZE=25</code> (1–100)</li><li><code>CRAWLER_RAM_SOFT_PERCENT=75</code></li><li><code>CRAWLER_RAM_HARD_PERCENT=85</code></li><li><code>CRAWLER_WORKER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900</code></li><li><code>CRAWLER_BLOCK_MEDIA=true</code> blocks main-page image/video/font downloads.</li></ul><p>Thresholds use total container cgroup memory where available, otherwise total host usage. They are cooperative guards, not a reservation or guarantee against sudden OOM. At soft pressure the worker checkpoints and exits. Hard pressure cancels work; the supervisor terminates an unresponsive owned process tree after 10 seconds. No-progress attempts stop after three retries with partial results preserved. An unrelated workload keeping memory above the soft threshold prevents further workers from starting.</p><p>Keep output/checkpoint files on a disk-backed persistent volume. Do not move SQLite output to <code>/dev/shm</code>; it counts against container RAM. Temporary comment spools are removed on normal exit; abrupt OS/container kills can leave <code>.crawl-comments-*</code> directories. Remove orphan spools only after confirming no active worker owns them. Preserve checkpoints for runs that may resume; apply the existing retention policy to completed runs.</p><p>Restarting the same output/run identity resumes its checkpoint without duplicate post IDs. Starting a new run ID starts independent work. Existing comment limits and late high-like comment ranking remain unchanged. Native JSON-array publishing reads batches of 25; the legacy object-envelope input retains its compatibility reader.</p><h2 id="validation-before-deployment" tabindex="-1">Validation before deployment <a class="header-anchor" href="#validation-before-deployment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Validation before deployment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Local tests cover 250 synthetic posts, separate child PIDs, crash recovery, orphan cleanup, memory-pressure cancellation, and bounded export allocation (20 MB output, under 4 MB Python peak allocation). These are not live Chromium/Facebook RSS measurements.</p><p>On a test server, run 50 → 150 → 300 posts with one crawl at a time. Record container <code>memory.current</code>/<code>memory.max</code>, host available memory, OOM events, worker exit codes, completed/pending counts, and peak Chromium RSS. Verify worker PIDs disappear after every batch and results have no duplicate/missing IDs. The feed phase still navigates Facebook&#39;s scrolling page: if its DOM cannot fit, partial feed is checkpointed and bounded retries eventually fail visibly; a durable Facebook pagination cursor is not implemented. Do not report such a run as complete.</p><p>No container limit was increased. <code>crawler-management.compose.yaml</code> specifies 4608m, while <code>backend.compose.json</code> specifies 5g; inspect the actual deployed container before sizing memory. Increasing RAM alone does not correct accumulation. Rollback switch: <code>CRAWLER_ISOLATED_BATCHES=false</code> restores the legacy in-process path and its previous memory risks.</p><p>Production update (2026-11-05): deployed and verified; see deployment-2026-11-05.md. Docker init is required to reap orphan Chromium processes; both production backend Compose and the crawler overlay now enable it. A real-browser 26-post synthetic smoke passed, including zombie cleanup; actual Facebook scale validation remains pending.</p>',12)])])}const h=o(n,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,h as default};
